EXT. BAKER'S YARD - PUDDING LANE, LONDON - NIGHT - 1666

A narrow courtyard hemmed by timber houses leaning toward each other like drunken gossips. The oven door glows red. Upwind, over the rooftops, the sky has begun to bloom a dull and sullen orange.

THOMAS FARRINER (50s, baker, flour to the elbow) stands at the oven mouth with a wooden peel, sliding out the last loaf of the night. His wife ALICE FARRINER (40s, shawl over a nightdress) bangs the shutters closed one by one. Their daughter MARGERY (12, barefoot, hair undone) clutches a cat to her chest by the well. The apprentice JEREMIAH PICKLE (16, soot-faced) hauls buckets across the cobbles, slopping water. Beside the gate, a neighbor, WIDOW CATHERINE MOLINEUX (60s, nightcap, rosary), stands with her mouth moving in silent prayer. And out of the shadows, a stranger on a gray horse, the King's messenger, SIR HUGH WAINWRIGHT (40s, mud to the thighs, a sealed letter in his gauntlet), reins in at the gate.

WAINWRIGHT
Master Farriner! By the King's order, put out your oven.

THOMAS
It's out, sir. Near enough.

WAINWRIGHT
Near enough has burned a parish.

Thomas turns to Jeremiah, who has frozen with a bucket in each hand. Alice drops the last shutter-bar. Margery tightens her grip on the cat, which squirms and yowls.

ALICE
Thomas.

THOMAS
I hear.

ALICE
Look at the sky.

He does not look. He keeps his eyes on the horseman. The widow's rosary clicks. The orange in the sky deepens; a distant bell begins to toll, then another, then three at once.

WAINWRIGHT
The fire is at Fish Street. It will be at the Bridge by dawn.

MARGERY
Papa, are we to leave?

THOMAS
We are not.

ALICE
Thomas.

THOMAS
We are not leaving my oven.

WAINWRIGHT
Master Farriner, I have orders.

THOMAS
Then read them.

Wainwright breaks the seal and reads silently. Thomas wipes his hands on his apron, and Alice, seeing it, steps forward and takes the loaf from the peel and presses it into Margery's arms beside the cat. Jeremiah sets down the buckets and begins, without being told, to untie the oven's iron door.

WAINWRIGHT
(folding the paper)
The order is plain. Quit the lane. All of you.

THOMAS
(quietly)
Where do we go, sir?

WAINWRIGHT
North. Moorfields.

Silence. The bells swell. A glowing ember lands on the stones at Catherine's feet. She crosses herself and steps on it.

CATHERINE
God keep this house.

ALICE
He will, Catherine. Come with us.

CATHERINE
I'll bring my chair.

Thomas takes one last look at the oven. Its mouth glows like an eye closing. He lays the peel across the sill, and lifts the latch of the gate.

THOMAS
Jeremiah. Take the handcart.

JEREMIAH
Yes, Master.

Thomas offers Alice his arm. She takes it. They file out into the lane, the cat wailing, the loaf warm in Margery's arms, and the orange glow licking down the rooftops behind them.
